What the AGPL actually does, mechanically
-----------------------------------------

The AGPL is the GNU General Public License version 3 with one additional
requirement bolted on, and it is worth stating plainly what that requirement
is, because it is the entire reason to pick this license over any other.

Under an ordinary copyleft license like the GPL, the obligation to hand over
source code is triggered by *distribution* -- by giving somebody a copy of the
program. This means a company can take GPL code, modify it, run the modified
version on their own servers, sell access to it over a network, and never
distribute a single copy to anyone. No copies changed hands, so no obligation
was ever triggered. The users get the service and never get the source.

Section 13 of the AGPL closes that. If somebody modifies this code and then
lets people interact with it remotely over a network, that interaction itself
triggers the obligation: they must offer those users the corresponding source
of their modified version, through the network, at no charge. Running it as a
service is treated the same as handing out copies.

The other terms are the standard GPLv3 ones. Anybody may run, read, modify, and
redistribute this. If they redistribute it, or run a modified version as a
network service, they must do so under this same license, with source
available. They cannot add restrictions on top. There is no warranty. Their
patent rights in what they contribute pass along with the code.

The practical effect is that this work stays open in every direction it can
travel. Someone can build on it, including commercially; what they cannot do is
close it, or wrap it in a service and keep the improvements to themselves.

