Where the source lives, and why it is a mirror and not a link
--------------------------------------------------------------

Section 13 wants users of a network service to be able to get the source of the
version they are actually talking to. The conventional way to do that is a
"Source" link somewhere in the running program's interface.

I do not trust that shape. The link is served by the very system whose source
is in question, which means it proves nothing -- a system that has something to
hide can serve whatever source it likes behind its own link, and the user has
no way to tell. Self-attestation is not evidence.

So the mechanism here is a public mirror at a fixed, known address instead:

    https://github.com/gabrilend/ai-stuff

That address is independently reachable. Anybody can check it without going
through the running service, compare it against what the service claims, and
notice a discrepancy. It has to stay publicly readable for this to work; if it
ever goes private, the source needs to surface somewhere else public and this
file needs to say where.

Note that section 13's obligation binds whoever modifies this code and runs it
as a service. That is mostly other people, not me. Someone who takes this,
changes it, and puts it online owes their users the source of *their* version,
by the same standard -- a real, reachable, independently checkable copy.

